    <DataNode elementId="_2-PROPENYL-GLUCOSINOLATE" textLabel="sinigrin" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="PHZOWSSBXJXFOR-GLVDENFASA-M" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="350" centerY="400" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, zhang, SRI, caspi">|FRAME: 2-PROPENYL-GLUCOSINOLATE Sinigrin| is a |FRAME: HOMOMETHIONINE|-derived glucosinolate that is found in some plants of the |FRAME: TAX-3699| family such as Brussels sprouts, broccoli, and the seeds of black mustard (|FRAME: TAX-3710|). The enzyme |FRAME: EC-3.2.1.147| (myrosinase) degrades |FRAME: 2-PROPENYL-GLUCOSINOLATE| to |FRAME: CPD-12139|, which is responsible for the pungent taste of mustard oil and horseradish.</Comment>
